     "They express a life understood as a relationship of trust and loyalty. Polycarp’s refusal was not portrayed as a sudden decision based on emotion. It was presented as the continuation of a lifelong commitment.",
     "The precise meaning of the reference to eighty-six years has been discussed by scholars. It is often understood as referring to the length of his Christian service, though interpretations differ over whether it indicates his age or the duration of his Christian life.",
     "9.2 A Lifetime of Faithfulness",
     "Polycarp’s confession is powerful because it connects the moment of trial with the years that came before it.",
     "He had served the Christian community, taught believers, encouraged faithfulness, and remained committed to Christ.",
     "The trial placed that commitment under extreme pressure, but it did not create the commitment itself.",
