     "title": null,
     "text": "SECTION A — MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n1. Where was Tertullian from?\nA. Jerusalem\nB. Carthage\nC. Rome\nD. Alexandria\n2. What was Tertullian especially known for?\nA. Serving as a Roman emperor\nB. Establishing the first Christian university\nC. Defending Christianity through influential writings\nD. Leading the Roman military\n3. What was the principal purpose of Tertullian’s Apology?\nA. To argue that Christians should rule the Roman Empire\nB. To defend Christians against unfair accusations and treatment\nC. To replace the New Testament with philosophical writings\nD. To establish a new Roman religion\n4. What was one of Tertullian’s central arguments concerning Christians accused of wrongdoing?\nA. Christians should never be investigated.\nB. Christians should receive special treatment regardless of their actions.\nC. Accusations should be examined fairly rather than accepting guilt because of a person’s religious identity.\nD. Roman law should never apply to Christians.\n5. What is Christian apologetics?\nA. The practice of explaining and defending Christian beliefs\nB. The practice of refusing to answer questions\nC. The rejection of all historical study\nD. The worship of political authorities\n6. Why did Tertullian object to Christians participating in idolatrous practices?\nA. He believed that Christian identity had no connection to worship.\nB. He believed that allegiance to God should shape Christian conduct.\nC. He believed that every cultural activity was necessarily sinful.\nD. He believed that Christians should avoid all relationships with non-Christians.\n7. What principle did Tertullian emphasize when challenging accusations against Christians?\nA. Rumors should always be accepted.\nB. Popular opinion is more important than evidence.\nC. People should be judged fairly according to the facts.\nD. Religious identity makes evidence unnecessary.\n8. Which work addressed Tertullian’s concerns about Praxeas and the relationship between the Father and the Son?\nA. Against Praxeas\nB. The Confessions\nC. The City of God\nD. The Shepherd of Hermas\n9. What does the Latin expression una substantia, tres personae summarize in Tertullian’s theological language?\nA. Three unrelated gods\nB. One divine substance and three persons\nC. Three separate Christian churches\nD. One human being with three physical bodies\n10. Which movement became associated with Tertullian during his later years?\nA. The New Prophecy movement, commonly called Montanism\nB. The Protestant Reformation\nC. The Franciscan movement\nD. The medieval scholastic movement\nSECTION B — TRUE OR FALSE\n11. Tertullian lived in Carthage in Roman North Africa.\n12. Tertullian believed that Christians should be condemned simply because they were Christians.\n13. Tertullian used his writing abilities to challenge accusations against Christian communities.\n14. Christians throughout the Roman Empire experienced one uninterrupted and identical policy of persecution during Tertullian’s entire lifetime.\n15. Christian apologetics should help people understand Christian beliefs rather than merely encourage arguments.\n16. Tertullian believed that Christian identity should influence everyday conduct.\n17. Every cultural practice must automatically be rejected by Christians.\n18. Tertullian contributed to the development of early Latin Christian theological language.\n19. Every position held by Tertullian was accepted by all later Christian traditions.\n20. Christians should combine courage in defending their beliefs with humility and a willingness to learn.\nSECTION C — SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n21. Describe the cultural and religious environment in which Tertullian lived.\n22. Why did some Roman citizens view Christians with suspicion?\n23. Explain the central argument of Tertullian’s Apology.\n24. What is Christian apologetics, and why is it important?\n25. How can Christians remain faithful to God while participating responsibly in society?\n26. Explain why Christian conduct matters when believers defend their faith.\n27. What contribution did Tertullian make to early Christian theological language?\n28. What was the New Prophecy movement, and why is it relevant to Tertullian’s story?\n29. Why should Christians evaluate the teachings of influential Christian writers carefully?\n30. What are three important lessons modern believers can learn from Tertullian?\nSECTION D — PERSONAL APPLICATION\n31. How would you respond if someone made an inaccurate accusation about your Christian faith?\n32. What can you do to become better prepared to answer difficult questions about Christianity?\n33. How can you remain faithful to your convictions without treating people who disagree with you disrespectfully?\n34. What should you do when you are uncertain whether a cultural activity conflicts with biblical teaching?\n35. How can you use your personal abilities to help others understand the gospel?"
