     "His later interpretation was theological and moral. He wanted to understand how a person could take pleasure in doing something wrong, even when the act itself offered little practical benefit.",
     "The story is not important because stealing pears was the greatest wrongdoing imaginable. It matters because Augustine used an ordinary incident to examine the deeper workings of human desire.",
     "He asked why people sometimes do wrong simply because they want to, because others are doing it, or because the act offers a sense of rebellion.",
     "His reflections anticipated questions that remain familiar today.",
     "Why do people repeat habits they know are harmful? Why is it sometimes easier to recognise a problem than to change it? Why can social pressure make wrongdoing seem attractive? Why do good intentions sometimes fail to produce consistent action?",
