     "text": "Section A — Multiple-Choice Questions\n1. Around which year was Jerome born?\nA. 250 AD\nB. 313 AD\nC. 347 AD\nD. 420 AD\n2. Where was Jerome born?\nA. Bethlehem\nB. Stridon\nC. Alexandria\nD. Constantinople\n3. Which languages were particularly important to Jerome’s biblical scholarship?\nA. Latin, Greek, and Hebrew\nB. Latin, Egyptian, and Persian\nC. Greek, Chinese, and Hebrew\nD. Latin, Arabic, and Sanskrit\n4. Which pope was associated with Jerome’s work in Rome?\nA. Pope Leo I\nB. Pope Gregory I\nC. Pope Damasus I\nD. Pope Innocent III\n5. Which Christian woman supported Jerome’s scholarly work in Bethlehem?\nA. Paula\nB. Perpetua\nC. Helena\nD. Thecla\n6. What was the Septuagint?\nA. A Latin translation produced by Jerome\nB. An ancient Greek translation of Jewish Scriptures\nC. A collection of Jerome’s letters\nD. A Roman legal code\n7. What was one major contribution Jerome made to the Latin Bible?\nA. He created the first Christian community.\nB. He revised Latin Gospel texts and translated much of the Old Testament from Hebrew.\nC. He wrote every book of the New Testament.\nD. He replaced the Hebrew Scriptures with Roman philosophy.\n8. What is the Vulgate?\nA. A Latin biblical text that became influential in western Christianity\nB. A collection of Roman military records\nC. A book written by Augustine\nD. A Greek commentary on Revelation\n9. Why did Jerome study Hebrew?\nA. To become a Roman government official\nB. To understand the Old Testament in its original language\nC. To replace all existing Christian writings\nD. To learn Roman law\n10. Where did Jerome spend much of his later life?\nA. Rome\nB. Jerusalem\nC. Bethlehem\nD. Athens\n11. Which activity formed a significant part of Jerome’s scholarly work?\nA. Writing biblical commentaries\nB. Commanding military campaigns\nC. Governing the Roman Empire\nD. Building Roman roads\n12. Why should the history of the Vulgate be understood as a process rather than a single event?\nA. Jerome never worked on biblical texts.\nB. Latin biblical texts existed before Jerome, and later copying and revision shaped the Vulgate.\nC. The Vulgate was written entirely in Greek.\nD. No one used the Vulgate after Jerome’s death.\n13. What does Jerome’s work teach about translation?\nA. Translation requires no interpretation.\nB. All languages express every idea in exactly the same way.\nC. Accurate translation requires careful attention to language and context.\nD. Familiar wording is always more accurate than source-language study.\n14. Which statement best describes Jerome’s historical legacy?\nA. He was responsible for writing the entire Bible.\nB. He made important contributions to biblical translation and scholarship.\nC. He was the first person to read the Bible.\nD. He never experienced disagreement with other Christians.\n15. What is one important lesson from Jerome’s life?\nA. Knowledge should be used to serve others.\nB. Learning is unnecessary for Christian service.\nC. Scholars should never question earlier translations.\nD. Personal recognition is the highest goal of education.\nSuggested Answers — Section A\n1. C — 347 AD\n2. B — Stridon\n3. A — Latin, Greek, and Hebrew\n4. C — Pope Damasus I\n5. A — Paula\n6. B — An ancient Greek translation of Jewish Scriptures\n7. B — He revised Latin Gospel texts and translated much of the Old Testament from Hebrew.\n8. A — A Latin biblical text that became influential in western Christianity\n9. B — To understand the Old Testament in its original language\n10. C — Bethlehem\n11. A — Writing biblical commentaries\n12. B — Latin biblical texts existed before Jerome, and later copying and revision shaped the Vulgate.\n13. C — Accurate translation requires careful attention to language and context.\n14. B — He made important contributions to biblical translation and scholarship.\n15. A — Knowledge should be used to serve others.\nSection B — Short-Answer Questions\n1. Describe Jerome’s early life and education.\n2. Explain how his spiritual journey influenced his later work.\n3. Why did Jerome travel to the eastern Mediterranean?\n4. What role did Pope Damasus I play in Jerome’s career?\n5. Why were existing Latin biblical translations sometimes difficult to use consistently?\n6. Explain why Jerome studied Hebrew.\n7. What was the Septuagint, and why did it matter to early Christians?\n8. Describe Jerome’s contribution to the development of the Vulgate.\n9. Explain the roles of Paula and Eustochium in the Christian learning environment surrounding Jerome.\n10. What is a biblical commentary, and how did Jerome contribute to this tradition?\n11. Why should Jerome’s work be understood within the historical context of the fourth and fifth centuries?\n12. How did Jerome’s scholarship influence western Christianity?\n13. Why is it inaccurate to say that Jerome single-handedly created every part of the Latin Bible?\n14. Identify three challenges associated with translating Scripture between languages.\n15. Explain why historical honesty and humility are important when studying influential Christian leaders.\nSection C — Deeper Reflection\n1. How can knowledge become a form of Christian service?\n2. Why is it important to learn from people whose backgrounds differ from our own?\n3. How can Christians distinguish between a faithful translation and an interpretation that goes beyond the available evidence?\n4. What does Jerome’s work teach about patience and long-term commitment?\n5. How can a person defend important convictions without becoming unnecessarily hostile?\n6. Why should Christians recognise the contributions of people who support important work behind the scenes?\n7. How can you use your education, language skills, or technical abilities to help others?\n8. What is the difference between respecting a historical figure and treating that person as infallible?\n9. How can careful study strengthen faith rather than simply produce arguments?\n10. What kind of legacy would you like your learning and service to leave?"
