     "title": null,
     "text": "PART A — MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n1. In which city did Athanasius serve as bishop?\nA. Jerusalem\nB. Alexandria\nC. Rome\nD. Antioch\n2. Who was Arius?\nA. A Roman emperor\nB. A Christian presbyter associated with a controversy over the Son’s relationship to the Father\nC. A monk who founded a monastery in Egypt\nD. The bishop who succeeded Athanasius\n3. In which year did the Council of Nicaea take place?\nA. AD 250\nB. AD 313\nC. AD 325\nD. AD 381\n4. What was Athanasius’s position at the Council of Nicaea?\nA. Emperor\nB. Bishop of Rome\nC. Deacon associated with Alexander of Alexandria\nD. President of the council\n5. What does homoousios mean in the Nicene confession?\nA. Created before all other creatures\nB. Of the same substance or one being with the Father\nC. A separate god with a different nature\nD. A title reserved only for bishops\n6. In what year did Athanasius become bishop of Alexandria?\nA. AD 303\nB. AD 325\nC. AD 328\nD. AD 350\n7. Where was Athanasius sent during his first major exile?\nA. Trier\nB. Jerusalem\nC. Constantinople\nD. Carthage\n8. Which work by Athanasius is especially associated with his explanation of the incarnation?\nA. Confessions\nB. On the Incarnation\nC. The City of God\nD. The Didache\n9. Which Christian ascetic’s life did Athanasius describe in an influential work?\nA. Benedict\nB. Antony\nC. Patrick\nD. Jerome\n10. In which year did Athanasius die?\nA. AD 325\nB. AD 356\nC. AD 373\nD. AD 381\nPART B — SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n1. Describe the historical and religious environment in which Athanasius lived.\n2. What was the central theological issue in the controversy associated with Arius?\n3. What did the Council of Nicaea affirm about the Son?\n4. Why did Athanasius believe the divinity of Christ mattered for salvation?\n5. How did political authority affect Athanasius’s ministry?\n6. Why did Athanasius spend time among Egyptian monks?\n7. What was the significance of On the Incarnation?\n8. How did Athanasius contribute to the Nicene movement?\n9. Why is it inaccurate to say Athanasius participated in the Council of Constantinople in AD 381?\n10. What is one important lesson modern Christians can learn from Athanasius?\nPART C — DEEPER REFLECTION QUESTIONS\n1. Why did Athanasius believe that the divinity of Christ was essential to the Christian understanding of salvation?\nSuggested Reflection: Athanasius believed that humanity needed more than moral instruction. Humanity needed restoration from sin and death, and he understood the incarnation of the eternal Word as God’s action to accomplish that restoration. His theology connected Christ’s identity with the meaning of salvation.\n2. What can Christians learn from Athanasius’s repeated exiles without assuming that suffering automatically proves a person is correct?\nSuggested Reflection: His life demonstrates that conviction can be costly and that political power can affect religious leadership. However, suffering alone does not prove a person’s arguments. Convictions must still be examined through Scripture, reasoning, historical evidence, and responsible discussion.\n3. How should Christian communities respond when political authorities attempt to influence theological decisions?\nSuggested Reflection: Christian communities should understand their responsibilities carefully, consider the legitimate role of civil authority, and avoid allowing political convenience to replace faithful theological judgment. Leaders should act with wisdom, courage, and accountability.\n4. Why is it important to understand the arguments of people with whom we disagree?\nSuggested Reflection: Understanding another position accurately makes meaningful discussion possible. It helps prevent caricature, reveals the real issues at stake, and allows people to explain their own convictions more clearly.\n5. How can Christian doctrine influence everyday discipleship?\nSuggested Reflection: Doctrine shapes how believers understand God, worship, salvation, and their responsibilities toward others. If theological knowledge remains disconnected from humility, love, worship, and obedience, it has not fully shaped a person’s life.\n6. What can Athanasius’s relationship with Egyptian monastic communities teach us about Christian support networks?\nSuggested Reflection: People facing pressure often need more than personal determination. Supportive communities can provide shelter, encouragement, prayer, practical help, and opportunities to continue meaningful work.\n7. Why should Athanasius’s legacy be understood alongside the contributions of other Christian leaders?\nSuggested Reflection: Christian doctrine developed through the work of many people over time. Athanasius made an important contribution, but other theologians continued to clarify the Church’s teaching. Recognizing this shared work gives a more accurate picture of Christian history."
