     "In response to the controversy, the Council of Nicaea met in AD 325. The council rejected Arius’s teaching and affirmed that the Son is of the same divine essence as the Father. Yet the controversy did not disappear after the council. Disputes continued for decades, and different bishops and emperors supported competing theological positions.",
     "These divisions affected local congregations. They were not merely arguments between distant scholars. They influenced church leadership, worship, public gatherings, and relationships among Christians.",
     "Milan was also caught up in these disputes. Its previous bishop, Auxentius, had been associated with the non-Nicene theological position commonly described as Arian.",
     "When Auxentius died in AD 374, the question of who would succeed him became a serious matter.",
