     "Milan stood among the most important cities of the Western Roman Empire. Its strategic location, administrative importance, and connection to imperial government made it a center of political activity, commerce, and public life.",
     "During the fourth century, the Roman Empire was experiencing significant religious transformation. Christianity had moved from an often-persecuted faith to a legally recognized religion, and Christian communities were becoming increasingly influential.",
     "However, the church’s growing influence also brought new challenges. Christian leaders had to determine how they should relate to rulers who possessed enormous political and military power.",
     "Some rulers supported Christianity, while others continued to follow traditional Roman religious practices. Even emperors who identified themselves with Christianity could make decisions that conflicted with Christian moral teaching.",
     "Milan became an important setting for these developments. Imperial officials, Christian bishops, wealthy citizens, and ordinary believers all participated in the city’s religious and political life.",
     "It was into this environment that Ambrose rose to prominence.",
     "Before becoming a bishop, Ambrose understood the workings of government. He knew that public authority could protect communities and maintain order, but he also understood that political influence could become dangerous when separated from justice and moral responsibility.",
     "His experience in public administration would later become valuable in his ministry.",
     "Ambrose would have to navigate a world in which the church could no longer be understood simply as a persecuted minority standing outside political power. Christian leaders now faced the temptation to become dependent on rulers or to use their influence for personal advancement.",
     "Ambrose’s ministry would demonstrate that the church’s responsibility was not merely to enjoy political favor. It also had to preserve its spiritual integrity.",
     "Practical Application",
