   "Introduction",
   "Gregory of Nazianzus was one of the most influential Christian teachers of the fourth century. Known for his deep understanding of Christian doctrine, powerful preaching, and careful explanation of the Trinity, he helped the Church defend its understanding of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit during a period of intense theological conflict.",
   "Gregory was born in Cappadocia, in Asia Minor, and received an excellent education. He developed a close friendship with Basil of Caesarea, another important Christian leader. Although Gregory often desired a quiet life devoted to prayer and study, the needs of the Church repeatedly drew him into public ministry.",
   "At a time when Christians disagreed about the identity of Jesus Christ and the divinity of the Holy Spirit, Gregory used his knowledge of Scripture and his gift for speaking to explain the Christian faith. His theological orations became particularly influential, and his leadership in Constantinople contributed to the wider recognition of Nicene Christianity.",
   "Gregory's life reminds believers that knowledge must be joined with humility, spiritual discipline, courage, and love. His example also demonstrates the importance of explaining biblical truth carefully rather than allowing confusion, fear, or personal ambition to control Christian teaching.",
   "Story Focus: Gregory's journey from education and friendship to theological leadership, his defense of the Trinity, his role in Constantinople, and the lasting influence of his teaching.",
   "Primary Scriptures: Matthew 28:19; John 1:1–14; John 14:16–17; John 16:13–15; 2 Timothy 2:15; Ephesians 4:1–6.",
   "Memory Verse",
   "2 Timothy 2:15 (KJV)",
   "“Study to shew thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth.”",
   "Memory Challenge: Memorize the verse and explain in your own words how it relates to Gregory's commitment to studying and teaching Christian doctrine.",
   "Final Assignment",
   "Title: Defending Truth with Wisdom and Humility",
   "Prepare a written reflection of approximately 500–800 words addressing the following:",
   "1. Summarize Gregory of Nazianzus's life and his contribution to the Church.",
   "2. Explain why the doctrine of the Trinity was important during his lifetime.",
   "3. Describe how Gregory's education and friendship with Basil influenced his ministry.",
   "4. Discuss what his resignation from Constantinople teaches about Christian leadership.",
   "5. Identify three principles from Gregory's life that you can apply to your own spiritual growth.",
   "6. Explain how you would help a new believer understand the importance of studying Scripture carefully.",
   "Practical Challenge: Over the next seven days, study one biblical passage each day. Record the main message, the questions it raises, and one practical way to apply it. Share one of your reflections with another believer and invite them to study the passage with you.",
   "Closing Prayer",
   "Heavenly Father,",
   "Thank You for the life and example of Gregory of Nazianzus. Thank You for the wisdom, courage, and dedication that helped him serve the Church during a difficult period in Christian history.",
