     "text": "Section A — Understanding the Story\n1. Where was Gregory of Nazianzus born?\n2. What role did Gregory's parents play in his early Christian formation?\n3. Which important Christian leader became Gregory's close friend?\n4. Name two major centres of learning where Gregory studied.\n5. Why did Gregory sometimes struggle with the responsibilities of public ministry?\n6. What was the Arian controversy primarily concerned with?\n7. What did the Council of Nicaea affirm about the Son?\n8. What were Gregory's Theological Orations?\n9. Why did Gregory defend the full divinity of the Holy Spirit?\n10. What did Gregory mean when he argued that what Christ did not assume was not healed?\n11. Why was Constantinople an important place for Gregory's ministry?\n12. What was the significance of the Council of Constantinople in AD 381?\n13. Why did Gregory resign from his position in Constantinople?\n14. What did Gregory do during his later years?\n15. Why is Gregory remembered as one of the Cappadocian Fathers?\nSection B — Biblical Connections\n1. How does Matthew 28:19 relate to the doctrine Gregory defended?\n2. What does John 1:1–14 teach about the identity of Jesus Christ?\n3. How does 2 Timothy 2:15 challenge Christian teachers to prepare carefully?\n4. What does Proverbs 27:17 teach about the value of friendship?\n5. How does Ephesians 4:1–6 encourage believers to pursue unity?\n6. What do Hebrews 2:14–18 and John 1:14 reveal about Christ's humanity?\n7. How does 1 Peter 5:2–4 describe the attitude expected of spiritual leaders?\nSection C — Critical Thinking\n1. Why is it dangerous to separate theological knowledge from Christian character?\n2. What can Gregory's relationship with Basil teach modern Christian communities about friendship?\n3. How can a Christian teacher remain firm in doctrine while treating people with patience and respect?\n4. Why should leaders sometimes consider stepping away from a position?\n5. How can Christian communities distinguish genuine doctrinal disagreement from personal rivalry?\n6. What are the benefits and limitations of using historical Christian writings when studying Scripture?\n7. What can Gregory's life teach us about balancing solitude with public responsibility?\n8. How can you apply Gregory's example of careful study and communication in your own life?\nSection D — Personal Reflection\n1. Which part of Gregory's story challenged your understanding of Christian leadership most?\n2. What area of biblical or theological knowledge would you like to understand more deeply?\n3. How can you help another person grow in their understanding of Scripture?\n4. What personal ambition might interfere with your service to others?\n5. What practical step will you take this week to grow in wisdom, humility, or faithfulness?"
