    "title": "The Church After the Council of Nicaea",
    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "Scripture Focus: John 1:1–3; Colossians 1:15–20",
     "In AD 325, Christian bishops gathered at the Council of Nicaea to address a major theological controversy concerning the identity of Jesus Christ.",
     "Arius, a Christian presbyter from Alexandria, taught that the Son was not eternal in the same way as the Father. In his understanding, the Son had been brought into existence by the Father and therefore was not equal to the Father in divinity.",
     "Other Christian leaders rejected this teaching. They argued that the Son was eternally divine and shared the Father's divine essence.",
     "The council rejected the central claims of Arius and affirmed that the Son was homoousios with the Father, a Greek term meaning that the Son shares the same essence or substance as the Father.",
     "The Nicene Creed expressed this conviction by confessing the Son as true God from true God.",
     "However, the council did not immediately end the controversy. Disagreements continued for decades. Different groups debated the meaning of theological terms, the interpretation of Scripture, and the implications of the council's decisions.",
     "Some bishops who supported Nicene Christianity faced opposition or removal from their positions. Others attempted to find alternative formulations that they believed could preserve Christian unity.",
     "Basil, Gregory of Nazianzus, and Gregory of Nyssa entered this complicated environment at different stages of their lives and ministries.",
     "Their contributions helped clarify why the Nicene confession mattered and how Christians could explain the relationship between the Father and the Son without teaching that they were either identical persons or separate gods.",
     "Their story reminds us that reaching an important decision does not always resolve every question. Sometimes a community must continue explaining, teaching, and applying its convictions for many years.",
     "Practical Application",
     "When you encounter a disagreement about Christian doctrine, identify the central question before deciding what you believe. Read the relevant biblical passages and examine the reasoning behind different interpretations."
    ],
