     "text": "SECTION A — UNDERSTANDING THE STORY\n1. Who were the three Cappadocian Fathers?\n2. Where was Cappadocia located?\n3. What major theological controversy followed the Council of Nicaea?\n4. What did the Council of Nicaea affirm about the Son?\n5. Why did theological disputes continue after AD 325?\n6. What was Basil the Great particularly known for?\n7. Why is Gregory of Nazianzus remembered as an influential theological teacher?\n8. What subjects did Gregory of Nyssa explore in his writings?\n9. What does the distinction between one divine essence and three persons mean in historic Trinitarian theology?\n10. Why did the divinity of the Holy Spirit become a subject of debate?\n11. What contribution did Basil make through On the Holy Spirit?\n12. How did the Cappadocian Fathers use Scripture and reasoning in their theological work?\n13. Why was the Council of Constantinople in AD 381 significant?\n14. How did the three Cappadocian Fathers contribute differently to the Church?\n15. Why does their theological legacy remain important today?\nSECTION B — BIBLICAL CONNECTIONS\n1. What does Matthew 28:19 reveal about the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit?\n2. How does John 1:1–14 contribute to the Christian understanding of Jesus Christ?\n3. What does John 15:26 teach about the Holy Spirit?\n4. How does 2 Timothy 2:15 encourage careful biblical study?\n5. What does Ephesians 4:1–6 teach about Christian unity?\n6. How does 1 Corinthians 12:12–27 explain the value of different gifts?\n7. What does 2 Corinthians 13:14 reveal about the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit?\n8. How does Acts 17:11 encourage careful examination of teaching?\nSECTION C — CRITICAL THINKING\n1. Why is it important to distinguish the divine essence from the three persons in historic Trinitarian teaching?\n2. How can Christians defend their beliefs without becoming hostile toward people who disagree?\n3. Why should theological terminology be used carefully?\n4. What can modern churches learn from the cooperation of the Cappadocian Fathers?\n5. Why is it important to study historical Christian writers in their original context?\n6. How can reasoning help Christian understanding while still recognizing the authority of Scripture?\n7. Why should the contributions of the wider Church be recognized when studying the Council of Constantinople?\n8. What dangers arise when people focus on winning theological arguments rather than understanding truth?\n9. How can Christian doctrine influence practical service and relationships?\nSECTION D — PERSONAL REFLECTION\n1. Which of the three Cappadocian Fathers' contributions interests you most, and why?\n2. What is one Christian doctrine you want to understand more clearly?\n3. How can you improve the way you study and interpret Scripture?\n4. Which abilities could you contribute to a shared Christian mission?\n5. How can you cooperate more effectively with people whose gifts differ from yours?\n6. What does this story teach you about humility in learning?\n7. How can you help another person understand a difficult biblical teaching?\n8. What practical step will you take this week to strengthen your knowledge, character, or service?"
