     "As the controversy continued, the relationship between religious conviction and political authority became increasingly complicated.",
     "Some Donatist communities resisted the authority of the Catholic Church and the decisions supported by imperial power. In parts of North Africa, the conflict became associated with social unrest and violence. The Circumcellions, a movement whose relationship with Donatism was complex, became involved in some episodes of confrontation.",
     "Imperial authorities increasingly used legal measures and coercion against Donatists. These actions included restrictions, confiscations, and efforts to suppress their communities.",
     "Augustine's position on coercion developed over time. He initially emphasised persuasion, but later came to defend certain forms of state pressure, believing they could bring people back into the Church. He appealed in part to the biblical language of compelling people to come in, found in Luke 14:23.",
     "His position remains one of the most debated aspects of his legacy.",
     "The use of political force against religious opponents raises serious questions about conscience, religious freedom, and the proper limits of state power. Augustine's later defence of coercion must be understood within the political circumstances of his time, but historical context does not remove the need to examine its consequences critically.",
     "The controversy reminds Christians that even when they believe they are defending truth and unity, the methods they choose can contradict the spirit of the Gospel.",
