     "Pelagius approached the matter differently.",
     "He did not believe that infants inherited original sin in the sense Augustine taught. He therefore did not accept that infant baptism was necessary to remove inherited guilt.",
     "Nevertheless, Pelagius acknowledged infant baptism as a Christian practice. The disagreement concerned its theological meaning and the condition from which infants were believed to need deliverance.",
     "The dispute revealed how a question about human nature could affect the understanding of a sacrament.",
     "If humanity was born into a fallen condition requiring divine grace, baptism could be understood as part of the Church's response to that need. If infants were not born bearing the consequences of Adam's sin in the way Augustine described, the rationale for infant baptism had to be understood differently.",
     "Christian traditions have continued to interpret baptism and original sin in different ways.",
     "The historical controversy reminds believers that practices of worship are often connected to deeper convictions about humanity, sin, grace, and salvation.",
