    "title": "A Small Movement in a Powerful Empire",
    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "The Roman Empire stretched across vast territories, connecting peoples, languages, cities, and cultures. Its roads linked distant provinces, its armies protected imperial borders, and its administrators maintained systems of government that influenced millions of lives.",
     "Within this powerful empire, a small movement began to spread.",
     "Its followers believed that Jesus of Nazareth, who had been crucified under Pontius Pilate, had risen from the dead. They proclaimed Him as Lord and Christ and announced that forgiveness and new life were available through Him.",
     "The earliest Christian communities emerged from the Jewish world of the first century. The apostles preached in Jerusalem and travelled to other regions, establishing communities of believers.",
     "The message gradually reached cities throughout the Roman Empire. In places such as Antioch, Ephesus, Corinth, and Rome, Christians gathered to pray, teach Scripture, share meals, and encourage one another.",
     "They did not begin as a powerful political institution. They had no imperial army and controlled no government.",
     "Their strength came from their conviction that Jesus Christ was alive and that His message was worth sharing.",
     "However, their faith also placed them in tension with aspects of Roman religious and social life. Christians refused to worship other gods and rejected practices they believed contradicted their allegiance to Christ.",
     "In a society where religious observances could be connected to public identity and civic loyalty, this refusal sometimes attracted suspicion.",
     "The Church's growth therefore took place in a complicated environment. Some Christians lived relatively peacefully, while others encountered hostility from neighbours, local authorities, or imperial officials.",
     "Persecution was not constant or identical across the empire. Its intensity varied by region and period.",
     "Nevertheless, the possibility of suffering became part of the experience of many early Christian communities.",
     "Practical Application",
     "Write a short reflection on what it means to follow Jesus when your convictions differ from the expectations of the people around you. Identify one situation in which you can demonstrate your faith through honesty, courage, and love."
    ],
