     "His correspondence, generally dated around AD 111–113, provides one of the most valuable surviving accounts of how a Roman official dealt with Christians.",
     "Pliny described questioning accused individuals about their beliefs and practices. Those who denied being Christians were sometimes required to demonstrate their denial by offering wine and incense before images of the emperor and the Roman gods.",
