    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "For much of the second century, Christian persecution remained uneven across the Roman Empire. Local hostility and legal proceedings could place believers in danger, but there was not always a single imperial campaign against all Christians.",
     "That situation changed dramatically under Emperor Decius.",
     "In AD 249–250, Decius introduced an empire-wide requirement for people to perform sacrifices to the Roman gods. The policy was associated with an attempt to restore traditional religious practices and strengthen the relationship between religious observance and imperial stability.",
     "People who complied could receive documentation confirming that they had performed the required sacrifice.",
     "For Christians, the requirement created a serious crisis of conscience.",
     "Those who refused to sacrifice risked punishment. Some were imprisoned, interrogated, or subjected to torture. Others attempted to avoid the authorities, while some complied with the demand.",
     "The Church faced a painful challenge.",
     "What should happen to Christians who had sacrificed under pressure? What about those who had obtained certificates without actually performing the sacrifice? How should congregations respond to believers who had failed?",
     "The crisis did not end when the immediate pressure subsided. It created disputes about repentance, discipline, and restoration.",
     "Some Christians believed that those who had compromised should face strict consequences. Others emphasised the possibility of repentance and readmission to the Church.",
     "These questions foreshadowed later controversies, including the Donatist controversy in North Africa.",
     "The persecution under Decius demonstrated that an imperial policy could affect Christians across many regions at once, creating both physical danger and serious challenges to the unity of the Church.",
     "Practical Application",
     "Reflect on how you would respond if a Christian friend admitted that fear had caused them to compromise an important conviction. Consider how you could encourage honest repentance without either excusing the failure or declaring the person beyond hope."
    ],
