     "text": "Answer the following questions in your own words. Use the historical narrative and the relevant biblical passages to support your understanding.\nSECTION A — HISTORICAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. How did Christianity begin to spread throughout the Roman Empire?\n2. Why did some Roman communities become suspicious of Christians?\n3. Why did Christians refuse to worship the traditional Roman gods?\n4. What happened during the fire of Rome in AD 64?\n5. How did Tacitus describe Nero's treatment of Christians?\n6. Who was Pliny the Younger, and why did he write to Emperor Trajan?\n7. What instructions did Trajan give Pliny concerning accusations against Christians?\n8. Who was Ignatius of Antioch, and why are his letters historically important?\n9. Who was Polycarp of Smyrna, and what is remembered about his death?\n10. How did Justin Martyr defend Christianity?\n11. What happened during the persecution under Decius around AD 249–250?\n12. What was the Great Persecution, and when did it begin?\n13. How did the persecution under Diocletian affect Christian communities?\n14. What changes took place under Galerius in AD 311?\n15. Why is AD 313 significant in the history of Christianity?\nSECTION B — THE EARLY CHURCH AND ITS WITNESS\n1. What does the word martyr originally mean?\n2. Why should martyrdom not be understood as seeking death?\n3. How did ordinary Christians contribute to the survival of the early Church?\n4. How did persecution affect the families of imprisoned or executed believers?\n5. Why did persecution sometimes create disagreements about repentance and restoration?\n6. How did early Christian communities support people who suffered?\n7. Why is it important to distinguish local persecution from empire-wide campaigns?\n8. How did Christian writings help preserve the memory of persecution?\n9. What challenges arose when Christianity gained imperial support?\n10. Why must the history of early Christianity be understood within its political and social context?\nSECTION C — BIBLICAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. What does Acts 5:29 teach about obeying God when human demands contradict His commands?\n2. How does Hebrews 13:3 encourage Christians to care for prisoners and people who are mistreated?\n3. What does Matthew 5:44 teach about responding to enemies?\n4. How does Romans 12:21 encourage believers to respond to evil?\n5. What does 1 Peter 3:15 teach about explaining Christian convictions?\n6. How can Christians combine respect for legitimate authority with faithfulness to God?\n7. Why is it important to respond to opposition without hatred or revenge?\n8. How can the teaching of Jesus guide believers who experience discrimination or hostility?\n9. What does Christian love require when another person is treated unjustly?\n10. How can prayer strengthen believers who face fear or uncertainty?\nSECTION D — CRITICAL THINKING\n1. Why is it inaccurate to claim that all Christians experienced the same degree of persecution throughout the Roman Empire?\n2. What can be learned from the correspondence between Pliny and Trajan?\n3. Why should ancient accounts of martyrdom be read with historical care?\n4. How can Christians remember the courage of martyrs without glorifying violence?\n5. What risks can arise when a religious community receives strong political support?\n6. How should Christians respond when their beliefs are misunderstood?\n7. What is the difference between defending religious freedom and demanding that everyone share the same faith?\n8. Why is practical care for persecuted families an important part of Christian witness?\n9. How can Christians defend truth while remaining compassionate towards their opponents?\n10. What lessons from the early Church can help Christians respond to religious intolerance today?\nSECTION E — PERSONAL APPLICATION\n1. What does faithfulness to Jesus mean to you when your convictions are challenged?\n2. How do you normally respond when people misunderstand your beliefs?\n3. What can you do to become better prepared to explain your Christian faith respectfully?\n4. How can you support someone who is suffering because of their beliefs?\n5. What is the difference between courage and unnecessary recklessness?\n6. How can you protect your conscience while respecting the dignity of people who disagree with you?\n7. What practical steps can your church take to support people facing persecution or discrimination?\n8. How can you avoid allowing fear to control your decisions?\n9. What can you learn from the ordinary believers whose names have not been preserved?\n10. What is the most important lesson you will carry from this topic into your Christian life?"
