   "Introduction",
   "For nearly three centuries, Christians in the Roman Empire experienced periods of suspicion, discrimination, and persecution. Some believers lost their property, some were imprisoned, and others were executed because they refused to abandon their faith in Jesus Christ. Yet Christianity continued to spread through families, cities, and communities.",
   "At the beginning of the fourth century, a dramatic change began to unfold. A Roman emperor named Constantine rose to power, and his decisions transformed the relationship between Christianity and the Roman government.",
   "In AD 313, Constantine and Licinius issued an agreement commonly known as the Edict of Milan, granting religious toleration and providing for the restoration of confiscated Christian property. Constantine subsequently supported Christian communities, sponsored church buildings, and became involved in disputes within the Church.",
   "These developments brought relief to many Christians, but they also introduced new challenges. What would happen when a faith that had sometimes been persecuted by political authorities began receiving imperial favour? Could the Church benefit from political support without becoming dependent on political power?",
   "This story explores Constantine's rise, the end of major imperial persecution, and the complicated relationship between Christian faith and earthly authority.",
