     "text": "Answer the following questions using the lessons from all fifteen chapters.\nSECTION A — HISTORICAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. What challenges did Christians face in the Roman Empire before Constantine?\n2. Who was Diocletian, and what was the Tetrarchy?\n3. What was the Great Persecution, and when did it begin?\n4. What kinds of punishment did Christians experience during the Great Persecution?\n5. How did Constantine begin his rise to imperial power?\n6. What happened at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in AD 312?\n7. Why do historical accounts of Constantine's reported vision require careful interpretation?\n8. What was the Edict of Milan, and when was it issued?\n9. What did the agreement provide concerning confiscated Christian property?\n10. Why is it historically inaccurate to say that the Edict of Milan made Christianity the official religion of Rome?\n11. What kinds of church-building projects did Constantine support?\n12. What controversy led to the Council of Nicaea in AD 325?\n13. What did the Council of Nicaea affirm concerning the Son's relationship to the Father?\n14. Why did disagreements continue after the Council of Nicaea?\n15. When was Constantine baptized, and when did he die?\n16. What important development occurred under Theodosius I in AD 380?\nSECTION B — BIBLICAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. According to Mark 10:42–45, how should Christian leadership differ from domination?\n2. What principle is expressed in Acts 5:27–32 concerning obedience to God?\n3. How does Romans 13:1–7 help Christians understand their relationship with governing authorities?\n4. Why should Romans 13 be read alongside other biblical teachings about truth, justice, and obedience to God?\n5. How does Matthew 22:15–22 help distinguish obligations to earthly authorities from obligations to God?\n6. What does 1 Peter 2:13–17 teach about respecting others and honouring authority?\n7. How can the example of Jesus in John 18:33–38 help Christians think about the difference between His kingdom and earthly political power?\nSECTION C — CRITICAL THINKING\n1. Why can political support benefit a church while also creating risks?\n2. What is the difference between religious freedom and forced religious conformity?\n3. Why should a Christian leader remain willing to correct a political authority when necessary?\n4. What dangers arise when a church measures spiritual success primarily through wealth, buildings, or public influence?\n5. Why should Christians defend freedom of conscience for people who hold different beliefs?\n6. What can the Church learn from Christians who remained faithful during persecution?\n7. How should Christians respond when they experience acceptance and influence rather than opposition?\n8. What is the difference between the kingdom of God and an earthly political empire?\n9. How can churches cooperate with governments without surrendering their spiritual integrity?\n10. Why should historical figures such as Constantine be evaluated with care rather than treated as perfect heroes or complete villains?\nSECTION D — PERSONAL REFLECTION\n1. What has this topic taught you about the relationship between faith and political authority?\n2. Which historical development in Constantine's reign did you find most significant, and why?\n3. What is one danger of depending too heavily on powerful people or institutions?\n4. How can you practise servant leadership in your family, church, school, or workplace?\n5. What would you do if a person in authority pressured you to act against your Christian convictions?\n6. How can you help protect the dignity and freedom of people whose beliefs differ from your own?\n7. What practical steps will you take to remain faithful to Christ in both difficult and favourable circumstances?"
