    "title": "Perpetua and Felicity: Courage in the Face of Persecution",
    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "During the early third century, Christians in parts of the Roman Empire continued to face persecution.",
     "One of the most well-known accounts of Christian martyrdom concerns Perpetua and Felicity, who suffered in Carthage, in North Africa, around AD 203.",
     "Perpetua was a young Christian woman from a family of some social standing. Felicity was an enslaved woman who was also a Christian.",
     "The surviving account of their imprisonment describes the challenges they faced as they prepared for execution because of their Christian faith.",
     "Perpetua's circumstances were especially difficult because she had a young child. Her family urged her to abandon her Christian confession, but she refused.",
     "The account also describes Felicity's pregnancy. According to the narrative, she gave birth while imprisoned, shortly before the execution of the group.",
     "The details of the story come from an early Christian martyrdom account, traditionally known as the Passion of Perpetua and Felicity. Some portions are presented as writings or visions associated with the martyrs, while other portions were supplied by a narrator or editor.",
     "The text must therefore be read as an early Christian account of martyrdom rather than as a modern court transcript.",
     "Nevertheless, it provides significant evidence of how Christians remembered and understood the courage of believers who suffered for their faith.",
     "Perpetua and Felicity became enduring examples of Christian steadfastness. Their different social circumstances also illustrate that faith in Christ brought together people from backgrounds that were sharply separated within Roman society.",
     "One was free and socially privileged; the other was enslaved. Both faced the consequences of confessing Jesus Christ.",
     "Their story reminds believers that faithfulness can demand extraordinary courage.",
     "At the same time, their example should not be used to glorify suffering for its own sake. Christians are not required to seek persecution or reject reasonable efforts to protect life. Their testimony concerns remaining faithful when confronted with coercion that demanded the abandonment of their convictions.",
     "Their memory became part of the Church's wider tradition of honouring martyrs whose courage strengthened other believers.",
     "Practical Application",
     "Read Hebrews 10:32–39.",
     "Reflect on the relationship between perseverance, hope, and faithfulness during hardship.",
     "Pray for people who face persecution because of their religious convictions, and consider how your church could support them through informed prayer, encouragement, or appropriate humanitarian assistance."
    ],
