     "text": "Answer the following questions using the lessons from all fifteen chapters.\nSECTION A — BIBLICAL AND HISTORICAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. Which women are mentioned in Luke 8:1–3 as accompanying and supporting Jesus?\n2. Why are the women who witnessed Jesus' crucifixion and visited His tomb important in the Gospel accounts?\n3. What role did Mary Magdalene play in the resurrection narrative in John 20?\n4. Who was Lydia, and where did Paul meet her?\n5. How did Lydia support Paul's missionary work?\n6. Who were Priscilla and Aquila?\n7. What did Priscilla and Aquila do when they encountered Apollos?\n8. Why is Priscilla's role in Acts 18:24–28 historically significant?\n9. What does Paul say about Phoebe in Romans 16:1–2?\n10. What are the possible meanings of the Greek term diakonos?\n11. What does Acts 9:36–42 tell us about Tabitha?\n12. What do the actions of the widows in Tabitha's story reveal about her ministry?\n13. Who were Philip's four daughters, and what does Acts 21:8–9 say about them?\n14. What does Philippians 4:2–3 reveal about Euodia and Syntyche?\n15. Who were Perpetua and Felicity, and why are they remembered?\n16. Who was Macrina the Younger, and how did she influence Christian life and thought?\n17. Who was Marcella of Rome, and what was she known for?\n18. How did Paula of Rome support Christian scholarship?\n19. What is meant by asceticism in early Christian history?\n20. Why is the historical record concerning women in the early Church incomplete?\nSECTION B — SCRIPTURAL UNDERSTANDING\n1. What do the Gospel accounts reveal about Jesus' treatment of women?\n2. How does Galatians 3:26–29 describe the identity of those who belong to Christ?\n3. What does 1 Corinthians 11:5 indicate about women praying and prophesying?\n4. Why should 1 Corinthians 11:5 be considered alongside other relevant passages when studying questions about women's ministry?\n5. What does Acts 18:24–28 teach about instruction and correction?\n6. What does Romans 16 teach about the different people who contributed to early Christian ministry?\n7. How does 1 Timothy 5:9–10 describe qualities associated with recognized Christian service?\n8. What does Titus 2:3–5 teach about older and younger women in the Christian community?\n9. How should Christians distinguish direct biblical evidence from later historical traditions?\n10. Why is the immediate context of a biblical passage important when drawing conclusions?\nSECTION C — CRITICAL THINKING\n1. Why should historians avoid assuming that every woman in the early Church had the same responsibilities?\n2. How can social class and legal status affect the historical experiences of women?\n3. What is the difference between a historical fact and a reasonable but unproven possibility?\n4. Why should modern Christians acknowledge harmful attitudes found in some historical Christian writings?\n5. How can churches recognize women's contributions without making unsupported historical claims?\n6. Why should historical accounts written by other people be evaluated carefully?\n7. How can believers disagree about women's ministry while treating one another with dignity?\n8. Why is practical service an important part of Christian discipleship?\n9. What can modern churches learn from the hospitality of Lydia and the service of Tabitha?\n10. How can Christians ensure that recognition of ministry is based on faithful service rather than social status?\nSECTION D — PERSONAL REFLECTION\n1. Which woman in the New Testament made the strongest impression on you, and why?\n2. What did you learn from the story of a woman in later Christian history?\n3. What practical contribution can you make to strengthen your church or Christian community?\n4. How can you encourage someone whose faithful service receives little recognition?\n5. What can you do to improve your understanding of the biblical and historical evidence concerning women in the Church?\n6. How can you respond respectfully when Christians interpret a difficult passage differently?\n7. What lesson from this topic will most influence your Christian life?\n8. How will you use your gifts, skills, resources, or time to serve others more faithfully?"
